-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

I am happy to to announce the long delayed release of version 0.15 of
diald.

Diald 0.15 fixes a few minor bugs that have been floating around for a
while annoying people. It also introduces a small number of new
features. Some that I added in, and several that were contributed by
users. See the CHANGES file in the distribution for for further
information.

For those who do not already know, diald is a daemon that provides on
demand IP networking using SLIP or PPP links.  The purpose of diald is
to make it transparently appear that you have a permanent connection to
a remote site.  Diald sets up a "proxy" device which stands in for the
physical connection to a remote site. It then monitors the proxy,
waiting for packets to arrive. When interesting packets arrive it will
attempt to establish the physical link to the remote site using either
SLIP or PPP, and if it succeeds it will forward traffic from the proxy
to the physical link. As well, diald will monitor traffic once the
physical link is up, and when it has determined that the link is idle,
the remote connection is terminated. The criteria for bringing the link
up and taking it down are configurable at run time, and are based upon
the type of traffic passing over the link.

The file is diald-0.15.tar.gz. As usual I've uploaded it to
sunsite.unc.edu:/pub/Linux/Incoming. It should move to
sunsite.unc.edu:/pub/Linux/system/Network/serial after a suitable
interval.  Diald can also be obtained from the diald home page at
"http://www.dna.lth.se/~erics/diald.html".  Notice the page has moved
from its old location at the university of Toronto. This is due to the
fact that I have moved.  There is a pointer at the old location to the
new location.

