newsgroups: comp.os.linux.announce


-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

The DMSDOS (compressed msdos) filesystem version 0.7.4 has just been
released.

It reads and writes the following filesystems:

	- Dos 6.0/6.2 DoubleSpace
	- Dos 6.22 DriveSpace
	- Win95 DoubleSpace/DriveSpace
	- Win95 Plus! DriveSpace 3 (still with some restrictions)
	- Stacker 3 (write without compression)
	- Stacker 4 (write without compression)

New in this release:

	- more speed (cluster caching added, optionally write-back)
	- less bugs (I hope :-))
	- Stacker writable (without compression, but this will
	  change some day)
	- compiles and runs with some 2.1.x kernels

Note: This software is still beta test code.

If you can't find it on the ftp servers listed below, try the incoming
directories.

Regards,
   Frank

Begin3
Title:		dmsdosfs
Version:	0.7.4
Entered-date:	01DEC96
Description:	dmsdos filesystem for Linux: extends msdos filesystem to allow
                full read and restricted write access to msdos/win95 compressed
                partitions (msdos files like dblspace.xxx and drvspace.xxx and
                stacvol.xxx). The following configurations are supported:
                 - DoubleSpace / DriveSpace compressed msdos 6.x partitions
                   (read-write),
                 - DoubleSpace and DriveSpace 3 compressed win95 partitions
                   (read-write with some restrictions for DriveSpace 3),
                 - and Stacker 3 and 4 compressed partitions (read-write,
                   but write currently without compression).
                This version is compatible with the 1.2.13, most 1.3.xx,
                pre-2.0.x and the 2.0.x kernels. Experimental support for
                2.1.x kernels has been added, but it may not work with brand
                new kernels. Dmsdos can run together with vfat
                or umsdos and can handle win95 long filenames itself. This
                version includes the new dmsdos daemon for fast write access.
                Support for Linux < 2.0.1 is likely to disappear soon.
